(2771629, 377899, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>First, this was a hard challenge, in my opinion.  I do like the idea - without reading the title but knowing the challenge, I''d have said that the "stupid" part might be paying $48 for whatever it was.  But with the title, and your explanation, AND being a fellow female, I do get it.  Unfortunately, it might not be so clear to the other half of the population (the male half), and they don''t get to read the comments until the challenge is over.<br/> <br/>As for the picture itself, you achieved excellent clarity on the tag itself, and I like the use of B&amp;W - it''s effective here.  Good use of natural light as well.  The finger on the left of the photo and the thumb look a bit out of place, though.  Perhaps having a bit more of the hand in the shot, or less shallow depth of field to keep the finger and thumb a bit more in focus might alleviate the "out of placeness".  <br/> <br/>By the way, the close up of the shoe you entered in "Superstitions" is very well done - but I see that a lot of people "didn''t get it" during the challenge.  You will find that the better your shots "meet the challenge" the better they''ll score.  You have a very interesting perspective on things - keep shooting!<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club First, this was a hard challenge, in my opinion.  I do like the idea - without reading the title but knowing the challenge, I''d have said that the "stupid" part might be paying $48 for whatever it was.  But with the title, and your explanation, AND being a fellow female, I do get it.  Unfortunately, it might not be so clear to the other half of the population (the male half), and they don''t get to read the comments until the challenge is over. As for the picture itself, you achieved excellent clarity on the tag itself, and I like the use of B&W - it''s effective here.  Good use of natural light as well.  The finger on the left of the photo and the thumb look a bit out of place, though.  Perhaps having a bit more of the hand in the shot, or less shallow depth of field to keep the finger and thumb a bit more in focus might alleviate the "out of placeness".   By the way, the close up of the shoe you entered in "Superstitions" is very well done - but I see that a lot of people "didn''t get it" during the challenge.  You will find that the better your shots "meet the challenge" the better they''ll score.  You have a very interesting perspective on things - keep shooting!', '2006-08-29 22:42:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(2771673, 378879, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>First, congratulations on a great score and great placement for your first challenge entry!  As for meeting the challenge, you definitely nailed it!  I think nearly everyone can relate to the action your image depicts, and I''m quite glad to hear that your hubby survived without requiring first aid!<br/> <br/>This image scored very well, despite as many have pointed out the lack of crisp, clear focus.  Composition is strong, with the knife forming a nice angle to both the hand and the pepper, and good placement of the subjects in the frame.<br/> <br/>The color is excellent, in both the red and the green of the pepper and the flesh tones in the hand.  The background has a bit of a yellow cast to it - if you use Photoshop (and it helps if you ask for critiques to post your editing steps in the comments), try doing a simple "Autolevels" - it really brightens up the background without altering the colors.  Also, since I''m not sure of your processing, a little sharpening will help a bit with the soft focus.<br/> <br/>Congrats on a great score, and keep shooting!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club First, congratulations on a great score and great placement for your first challenge entry!  As for meeting the challenge, you definitely nailed it!  I think nearly everyone can relate to the action your image depicts, and I''m quite glad to hear that your hubby survived without requiring first aid! This image scored very well, despite as many have pointed out the lack of crisp, clear focus.  Composition is strong, with the knife forming a nice angle to both the hand and the pepper, and good placement of the subjects in the frame. The color is excellent, in both the red and the green of the pepper and the flesh tones in the hand.  The background has a bit of a yellow cast to it - if you use Photoshop (and it helps if you ask for critiques to post your editing steps in the comments), try doing a simple "Autolevels" - it really brightens up the background without altering the colors.  Also, since I''m not sure of your processing, a little sharpening will help a bit with the soft focus. Congrats on a great score, and keep shooting!', '2006-08-29 22:57:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(2771868, 381269, 55956, '<td valign="top">*Greetings from critique club"<br/> <br/>I think that your husband it placed much attention at the blond woman :)<br/>The challenge title was stupid stupid and a shot in a party match the contest. good your attention for capture the moment. this isn''t a true artistic shot, but is difficult snapped an artistic shot in a contest with that title. I think that this photo is the maximum that you can obtain in that situation.<br/>best regards</td>', '*Greetings from critique club" I think that your husband it placed much attention at the blond woman :)The challenge title was stupid stupid and a shot in a party match the contest. good your attention for capture the moment. this isn''t a true artistic shot, but is difficult snapped an artistic shot in a contest with that title. I think that this photo is the maximum that you can obtain in that situation.best regards', '2006-08-29 23:55:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(2772028, 379036, 26906, '<td valign="top">Things that you mention in your critique really get me thinking if you should be in the Critique Club at all :) Just tell me, why would I want to eliminate a shadow on a girl''s face? As for the legs on the bath tub... It does not take a genius to come up with at least two ideas why are they there. Come on, flex your brain muscule.<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by atupdate:</b></div><hr/><i> Hello from the Critique Club,<br/> <br/>I remember when voting on this image that I thought you executed your idea very well but I was confused as to why the two people were standing in the background.  Reading through the comments you received, I see that those two people ended up being a love or hate type thing for those that left comments.  This probably holds true for the general voters as well and could have the reason why this image didnt break into the sixes.<br/> <br/>I would suggest the next time you take a picture with a strong side light that you use a reflector to fill in some of the shadows on the opposite side from the light source.  With a little fill light on the left side of the image, the shadows on the face of the girl in the bathtub could have been eliminated.  Congratulations on your top twenty finish and I look forward to seeing more of your entries in future challenges.<br/> <br/>Feel free to send me a PM if you have any questions regarding this critique.<br/> <br/>Tim </i></td></tr></table><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-08-30 11:05:54.</i></td>', 'Things that you mention in your critique really get me thinking if you should be in the Critique Club at all :) Just tell me, why would I want to eliminate a shadow on a girl''s face? As for the legs on the bath tub... It does not take a genius to come up with at least two ideas why are they there. Come on, flex your brain muscule. Originally posted by atupdate: Hello from the Critique Club, I remember when voting on this image that I thought you executed your idea very well but I was confused as to why the two people were standing in the background.  Reading through the comments you received, I see that those two people ended up being a love or hate type thing for those that left comments.  This probably holds true for the general voters as well and could have the reason why this image didnt break into the sixes. I would suggest the next time you take a picture with a strong side light that you use a reflector to fill in some of the shadows on the opposite side from the light source.  With a little fill light on the left side of the image, the shadows on the face of the girl in the bathtub could have been eliminated.  Congratulations on your top twenty finish and I look forward to seeing more of your entries in future challenges. Feel free to send me a PM if you have any questions regarding this critique. Tim ', '2006-08-30 00:41:29.000000', '2006-08-30 12:05:54.000000', 0),
